  "dependencyClosureNote": "Runtime import closure computed with the legacy source's own installed esbuild 0.25.12 metafile (bundle of the entry test at the pinned revision, platform node, format esm, vitest external), not a hand-written import regex. The prior regex-based closure missed (a) the multi-line import of src/shared/cli-argument-boundary.ts in src/cli/args.ts, which failed the first run-cli capsule before any test ran (preparation failure, retained as .preflight/parity-baseline/native-coord-run-cli-WvW5x8 with its stage receipt and failed vitest-results.json), and (b) the statically reachable zod / ws / tweetnacl imports via src/shared/runtime-rpc-envelope.ts, src/shared/runtime-environments.ts, src/shared/mobile-relay-pairing-offer.ts, src/shared/ephemeral-vm-recipes.ts, src/shared/remote-runtime-request-socket.ts, src/shared/remote-runtime-subscription-outbound.ts, src/shared/remote-runtime-subscription-transport.ts and src/shared/e2ee-crypto.ts. The staged set is the full runtime-reachable first-party closure of the entry test (type-only imports are erased by the TS transform exactly as at runtime; '.js' specifiers resolve to their '.ts' files; vi.mock literal targets resolve to files already in the closure). All other externals are node: builtins plus vitest itself; the three pinned third-party packages are declared in transportRuntime below and staged by the capsule runner as verified junction links into the legacy source's already-installed node_modules (read-only), with WS_NO_BUFFER_UTIL=1 and WS_NO_UTF_8_VALIDATE=1 so ws's optional native peer modules stay out of the pinned closure. The first-party file list was additionally confirmed empirically: the capsule executes with no missing-module error under the source's own pinned vitest.",
  "expectedTestCounts": {
    "files": 1,
    "tests": 12
  },
  "sourceVitestVersion": "^4.1.11",
  "sourceVitestConfigFile": "config/vitest.config.ts",
  "sourceVitestConfigDifferencesFromCapsule": [
    "source test.environment is 'node' (matches capsule; no difference here)",
    "source defines a global constant ORCA_FEATURE_WALL_ENABLED='true' at build time; the capsule's generated config defines nothing, since the pinned files never reference it",
    "source registers three setupFiles (config/scripts/happy-dom-offscreen-canvas.ts, config/scripts/happy-dom-mutation-observer-retention.ts, config/scripts/vitest-host-ports-setup.ts); the capsule registers none, since the pinned files touch no DOM/canvas/host-port surface",
    "source sets hookTimeout=60000ms and testTimeout=30000ms for its full, heavier suite; the capsule uses Vitest's built-in defaults",
    "source passes execArgv ['--no-experimental-webstorage','--expose-gc'] to the worker process for retention tests elsewhere in the suite; the capsule passes none, since the pinned files allocate nothing that a GC-timing test would observe",
    "the capsule executes the source project's own installed vitest 4.1.11 entry module (node_modules/vitest/vitest.mjs, read-only) under Node 24.19.0, so the earlier pilot's vitest 5.0.0 major-version compatibility gap does not apply to this capsule; the generated capsule config remains intentionally minimal as listed above"
  ],
  "isolationRationale": "The pinned handler modules are pure flag/RPC-payload mapping and output-formatting logic: no filesystem, network, child-process or timer side effects execute under these assertions. The transport client is an inline object supplied by the original source test itself (`client: { call: callMock }`), and five of the entry tests additionally vi.mock the sibling output/selector modules exactly as the original file does -- these are the source tests' own dependency substitutions at the CLI->transport and CLI->console seams, not mocks of the code under test. Every handler assertion runs against the real, unmodified handler modules. The pinned third-party transport packages (ws, tweetnacl, zod) load from junction links the runner creates into the legacy source's already-installed node_modules, read-only, with ws optional-native peers disabled via environment; no package is copied, installed or written. This is isolation by staging, not a security sandbox."
}
